{
  "comment": "Ciena Waveserver 5 optical threshold notifications, transcribed from CIENA-WS-NOTIFICATION-MIB.mib (LAST-UPDATED 201611140000Z, Release 1.3) in the kcsinclair/mibs mirror. Ciena models an optical alarm as ONE notification type, wsLinkStateAlarmNotification (1.3.6.1.4.1.1271.3.2.12), whose varbinds carry the condition state -- NOT as a raise/clear pair of distinct traps like linkDown/linkUp. So all four entries share one trap OID and differ only in Severity (.7) and which condition flag is active. Flags are inactive(0)/active(1) per the MIB; a summary claiming active(2)/inactive(1) was wrong. Severity is non-contiguous: cleared(1) critical(3) major(4) minor(5) warning(6) info(8). The full flag list is emitted literally, as the real device does, so a collector parsing the whole varbind set sees the same shape it would in production. NOTE: because a clear zeroes every flag, the SD clear and the SF clear are identical on the wire apart from Description (.9) and Instance (.8). That is Ciena's model, not an omission here -- setting a condition flag on a clear would assert active(1) and cleared(1) at once. See ciena_waveserver5_limitations.md, 'A clear names its condition only in text'. Wire-shape notes from review: varbind OIDs carry the .0 instance sub-identifier (what an SMIv2 agent emits; a bare object OID would never match a collector's instance-exact rule); EntityType is emitted LAST per the OBJECTS clause and set to linePort(3) -- an OTU pre-FEC alarm is a line-side condition; DateAndTime is a human-readable local timestamp ({{.NowLocal}}), the MIB type is a DisplayString date, not epoch seconds. Condition-flag ORDER follows numeric OID order, not the OBJECTS clause: the clause is internally inconsistent (names EthEBer/EthPcsLol where the defined objects are EthPcsHighBer/EthPmaSool), so the OBJECT-TYPE definitions are the transcription source of truth here.",
